        "e": "<strong>Inferior temporal cortex and visual agnosia:</strong> The ventral visual stream (\"what pathway\") projects from primary visual cortex (V1) through V2 and V4 to terminate in the inferior temporal (IT) cortex (including the fusiform and parahippocampal gyri). Neurons in IT cortex possess large receptive fields that span the visual field and respond selectively to complex, high-level geometric shapes, objects, and faces with position, size, and viewpoint invariance. Bilateral lesions to the inferior temporal cortex produce <i>visual object agnosia</i>—the inability to recognize, identify, or name common objects by sight (e.g., mistaking a fountain pen for a stick), despite intact visual sensation (normal visual acuity, visual fields, and color vision) and preserved ability to identify the object when touched or heard.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A describes optic ataxia or motion tracking deficits typical of dorsal stream parietal/MT lesions.<br>&bull; C describes the vestibulo-ocular reflex (VOR) mediated by vestibular nuclei and cranial nerve nuclei III/VI.<br>&bull; D describes reflexive visual orienting mediated by the superior colliculus and frontal eye fields."
