        "e": "<strong>Neuromuscular proprioceptors: Spindles vs Golgi Tendon Organs:</strong> The somatic motor system incorporates two primary mechanosensory proprioceptors that monitor muscle state:<br>1. <strong>Muscle Spindles:</strong> Located <i>in parallel</i> with extrafusal skeletal muscle fibers. They contain specialized intrafusal fibers innervated by Group Ia sensory afferents that detect changes in muscle <i>length</i> and rate of stretch. Tapping the patellar tendon stretches the quadriceps, activating Ia afferents that enter the spinal cord and form monosynaptic excitatory connections onto $\\alpha$-motor neurons innervating the same muscle, eliciting reflexive muscle contraction (the <strong>myotatic or stretch reflex</strong>).<br>2. <strong>Golgi Tendon Organs (GTOs):</strong> Located <i>in series</i> with extrafusal fibers at the musculotendinous junction. Innervated by Group Ib sensory afferents, GTOs are highly sensitive to <i>contractile muscle tension and force</i>. When muscle contraction generates extreme tension that threatens tendon integrity, Ib afferents fire and excite inhibitory interneurons in the spinal cord. These interneurons release glycine to inhibit the $\\alpha$-motor neurons of the contracting muscle, causing sudden muscle relaxation (the <strong>inverse myotatic reflex or autogenic inhibition</strong>).<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A inverts the structural alignment and reflexes of the two proprioceptors.<br>&bull; B attributes these spinal reflexes to cutaneous/joint mechanoreceptors.<br>&bull; C confuses motor neurons with sensory afferent proprioceptors."
